Title: A scheduler-level wedge control: a host.ensure that never resolves
Created: 2026-09-15
Author: haduong

--- log ---
2026-09-15T22:01Z haduong created

2026-09-15T22:04Z claude note Filed from 0793: the wedge control Johnson cannot be. Deferred out of 0793 because tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs was being edited concurrently for 0796.
--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0793 built `bench/sdt_stall_probe.py`: a plateau predicate over the
sitter's heartbeat journal, plus a per-thread CPU sampler, plus the verdict that
combines them. Both arms of the predicate are asserted against the preserved AR6
trace — silent while progress climbs 69 → 88, loud across the three beats pinned
at 90 — and the sampler is validated against a synthetic busy loop and a
sleeping process.

What is still missing is a control one level up: **a fabricated wedge inside the
scheduler itself**. `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` drives `sweep()` against a
mock host. A mock `host.ensure` that never resolves, with a fake clock advanced
past the heartbeat interval, would show the scheduler emitting exactly the beat
shape the AR6 produced — and therefore that the predicate fires on a wedge
manufactured end to end, not only on one historical JSON file.

Why it is a separate ticket and not part of 0793: `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`
was being edited concurrently for ticket 0796 while 0793's work was in flight,
and two agents on one test file is the contention the delegation rules say to
avoid. The 0793 work was scoped to `bench/` and `verification/` for that reason.

Why it is worth doing at all: the predicate's plateau arm currently rests on one
recorded incident. If the sitter's heartbeat fields ever change shape — a rename,
a different clock, a beat that stops firing when `active` is set — the fixture
keeps passing while the live predicate goes blind. A scheduler-level control is
the only thing that couples the predicate to the code that produces its input.

## Relevant files

- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` — where the control belongs; already has the
  mock-host and fake-clock machinery (see the existing heartbeat assertions).
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `sweep()`, the `host.ensure` await, and the
  comments on uninterruptibility; read, not changed.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `heartbeatTick()` builds `elapsedMS` and
  `sinceProgressMS`; the fields the predicate reads.
- `bench/sdt_stall_probe.py` — `analyse_heartbeats()`, the predicate under test.
- `tests/test_sdt_stall_probe.py` — the existing two-arm assertion over the
  recorded trace.
- `verification/incidents/0793-2026-09-15-ar6-stall.json` — the shape to
  reproduce.

## Actions

1. In `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`, add a case whose `host.ensure` returns a
   promise that never settles, with the fake clock advanced past several
   heartbeat intervals.
2. Assert the emitted heartbeats have the AR6 shape: `phase` stays `extracting`,
   `progress` frozen, `sinceProgressMS` growing by one interval per beat.
3. Assert the healthy counterpart too — a `host.ensure` that reports progress
   and settles — so the control discriminates rather than merely fires.
4. Cross-check the emitted beats against the Python predicate (export them as a
   fixture, or assert the same arithmetic in the JS test; whichever keeps one
   source of truth for the thresholds rather than two copies of them).

## Test

The test IS the deliverable here. Its own red step: run step 2's assertions
against the *healthy* mock first and watch them fail — a wedge control that also
passes on a document that finishes is not a control.

## Verification

- [ ] Wedge case emits beats whose `sinceProgressMS` grows by exactly one
      heartbeat interval, progress frozen
- [ ] Healthy case emits beats whose `sinceProgressMS` resets, progress climbing
- [ ] The predicate's verdict on each set of beats is asserted, not assumed
- [ ] Thresholds live in one place, not duplicated between the JS and Python sides

## Invariants

- No change to `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` or `bootstrap.js`: this adds a
  test, it does not change behaviour.
- The never-resolving promise must not leave the test runner hanging — the fake
  clock advances, the real one does not wait.
- `make check` passes.

## Exit criteria

- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` carries both the wedge and the healthy case,
  and the wedge case has been observed failing against the healthy mock before
  being made to pass.
- 0793's predicate is exercised against beats the scheduler produced, not only
  against a recorded file.
